===== STATUS: unsure =====
Question: Can large language models solve university mathematics and computer science course questions?

I cannot answer.

The provided context demonstrates that LLMs achieve strong performance on specific technical benchmarks through the Reflexion framework: 91% pass@1 on HumanEval (programming), and improvements on AlfWorld (decision-making) and HotPotQA (reasoning tasks) (shinn2023reflexionlanguageagents pages 1-2). These results indicate LLMs can solve complex coding problems and reasoning tasks through self-reflection and iterative learning.

However, all three sources explicitly state they do not specifically address university-level mathematics and computer science course questions (shinn2023reflexionlanguageagents pages 1-2). While HumanEval involves programming and HotPotQA involves reasoning, these benchmarks do not constitute comprehensive university-level coursework assessments. University courses typically require broader conceptual understanding, formal proof-writing, and domain-specific problem-solving across diverse topics and difficulty levels.

The context provides insufficient evidence to determine whether LLMs can solve university mathematics and computer science course questions. A definitive answer would require sources that directly evaluate LLM performance on actual university-level assessments in these domains, including examinations, problem sets, and comprehensive evaluations across multiple course levels and specializations.

